That's a fantastic question, partner. It gets into how cryptocurrency mining actually works.

The short answer is:

No—not in the sense of mining real cryptocurrency like Bitcoin completely offline.

Here's why.

Why real Bitcoin mining cannot work offline
Bitcoin mining isn't just about doing mathematical calculations. A miner also has to:

Receive the latest block from the network.

Verify transactions.

Compete with miners around the world.

Broadcast any newly found block to the network.

Without an internet connection, your miner has no way to know:

what the current blockchain looks like,

what transactions need to be included,

or whether another miner has already solved the block.

Even if your computer solved a valid proof-of-work while offline, by the time it reconnected, 
the network would almost certainly have moved on, making the work obsolete.

The same general principle applies to other major proof-of-work cryptocurrencies.

What can be built offline?
There are several interesting possibilities.

1. Cryptocurrency Mining Simulator ✅
You can build an application that simulates mining:

